## SCOPE NOTE

All three general search engines were blocked for this session (Brave 429 incl. via `r.jina.ai`, DDG 202-blocked, Mojeek + SearxNG captcha). Everything below comes from **direct primary sources**: spec.torproject.org, `gitlab.torproject.org` git blobs + REST API (commits, issues), blog.torproject.org permalinks, the Wayback CDX API, and **the live Tor network itself** (I fetched a live consensus and the live authority key-certificate bundle from maatuska). That means my incident coverage is good where a ticket/commit/changelog exists and weak where the only record would have been a mailing-list thread or a search-engine-indexed news article. Gaps are flagged explicitly at the end.

---

# 1. THE CRUX FIRST: THRESHOLD SIGNING, NOT SECRET SHARING

**Tor uses m independent RSA signatures on a byte-identical document. There is no secret sharing, no key splitting, and no threshold-signature scheme anywhere in the consensus path.** Confidence: **very high** — this is settled by four independent lines of evidence:

1. **The document format itself.** A consensus carries N separate `directory-signature <identity-digest> <signing-key-digest>` items, each an ordinary RSA signature over the same bytes by a different authority's own signing key. Spec: <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/consensus-formats.html> ("Vote and consensus document, signatures"). I confirmed this on the wire — the live consensus valid-after `2026-09-03 23:00:00` carries exactly 9 `directory-signature` lines, one per authority, fetched from `http://171.25.193.9:443/tor/status-vote/current/consensus`.

2. **The byte-identity requirement exists *because* it's countersigning, not threshold signing.** <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/consensus-formats.html> — "Consensus documents generated by each dirauth must be byte-for-byte identical, as the same document must be countersigned by multiple dirauths." Hence the tightened metaformat (each WS exactly one SP, no extra arguments, fixed item order). A threshold scheme would not need this.

3. **Signatures are exchanged as detached blobs, not combined.** <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/exchanging-detached-signatures.html> — each authority POSTs its detached signature to every other authority at `/tor/post/consensus-signature`. Nothing is reconstructed; signatures are merely *accumulated*.

4. **The spec explicitly lists threshold signatures as a road *not* taken.** In the shared-randomness security analysis, under "Security of commit-and-reveal and future directions," possible future directions include "Schemes based on threshold signatures (e.g. see [HOPPER])", Unicorn, and VDFs — i.e. as of the current spec they are hypothetical. (Full-book text at <https://spec.torproject.org/print.html>.)

The one place Tor does run a genuine multi-party protocol is the **shared random value** (commit-and-reveal, prop 250, `srv-spec`), and even that is commit-and-reveal — *not* secret sharing. `SRV = SHA3_256("shared-random" | INT_8(REVEAL_NUM) | INT_4(VERSION) | HASHED_REVEALS | PREVIOUS_SRV)`. Its threshold is a consensus parameter, `AuthDirNumSRVAgreements`, **default = 2/3 of the total number of dirauths** (min 1).

**Consequence of this design, stated by Tor themselves in 2008:** because signatures are independent, compromising k authorities gives you k signatures and nothing more — you cannot reconstruct anything. Roger Dingledine, 2008-05-14: *"if they could forge a new v3 networkstatus consensus, they could trick users into using their own fabricated Tor network… But fortunately, Tor clients require a majority of signatures before they'll believe the consensus — and that's four of six. (Whew, that was close!)"* — <https://blog.torproject.org/debian-openssl-flaw-what-does-it-mean-tor-clients/>

---

# 2. WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENED (dated, sourced)

### 2008-05-13 — Debian OpenSSL PRNG bug compromised **three of six** v3 authority identity keys
The single most important real key-compromise event. Tor 0.2.0.26-rc changelog (verified in `ReleaseNotes`, <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/raw/main/ReleaseNotes>):

> **o Major security fixes:** Use new V3 directory authority keys on the **tor26, gabelmoo, and moria1** V3 directory authorities. The old keys were generated with a vulnerable version of Debian's OpenSSL package, and **must be considered compromised**. Other authorities' keys were not generated with an affected version of OpenSSL.
>
> **o Minor features:** Add a new **V3AuthUseLegacyKey** option to make it easier for authorities to change their identity keys if they have to.

So: 3-of-6 authority *identity* keys (the supposedly-offline root, not just the signing key) were predictable, and the threshold at the time was 4-of-6. The margin was one signature. `V3AuthUseLegacyKey` — the dual-signing migration mechanism — and consensus method 3 ("Added legacy ID key support to aid in authority ID key rollovers") both exist because of this event. Confidence: **very high** (changelog text is verbatim). Roger's explainer: <https://blog.torproject.org/debian-openssl-flaw-what-does-it-mean-tor-clients/> (2008-05-14). ~300 of ~1500–2000 relays also had weak keys.

### 2011-03-10 — an experimental descriptor **took down the directory authorities**
Tor 0.2.2.23-alpha changelog: *"Fix an assert in parsing router descriptors containing IPv6 addresses. **This one took down the directory authorities when somebody tried some experimental code.** Bugfix on 0.2.1.3-alpha."* Same release also fixed a bridge-authority information leak (bridge descriptors returned to `/tor/server/all`, found by "piebeer"). Confidence: **high** (changelog verbatim; archived at <http://web.archive.org/web/2017/https://blog.torproject.org/category/tags/directory-authorities>). No post-mortem published.

### 2014-07-04 / 2014-07-30 — the "relay early" traffic-confirmation attack
<https://blog.torproject.org/tor-security-advisory-relay-early-traffic-confirmation-attack/> (arma, 2014-07-30). **This was a relay/Sybil attack, not a dirauth key incident** — worth stating plainly, since it is often filed under "Tor trust failures."
- Attacking relays joined **2014-01-30**, removed **2014-07-04**; users of onion services from early Feb to Jul 4 should assume they were affected.
- ~**115** fast non-exit relays on `50.7.0.0/16` and `204.45.0.0/16`, ≈**6.4%** of Guard capacity.
- Mechanism: encode the onion-service address as a sequence of RELAY vs RELAY_EARLY cells inbound (only outbound relay-early was counted, per the bug-1038 fix), read it back at the Guard position.
- Detected by the **DocTor** scanner when the relays joined; the set was judged not-large-enough at the time. Fixes: 0.2.4.23 / 0.2.5.6-alpha, plus the move toward **one entry guard** instead of three.
- **Aftermath:** 2015-11-11 — <https://blog.torproject.org/did-fbi-pay-university-attack-tor-users/> — CMU/SEI, *"payment to CMU was at least $1 million,"* no indication of a warrant or IRB oversight. 2016-02-24 — <https://blog.torproject.org/statement-tor-project-re-courts-february-23-order-us-v-farrell/> — Tor's statement in *U.S. v. Farrell*: *"The Tor network is secure and has only rarely been compromised. The Software Engineering Institute ('SEI') of Carnegie Mellon University (CMU) compromised the network in early 2014 by operating relays and tampering with user traffic."*

### 2014-09-29 → 2014-11-15 — **turtles** dropped for operator abandonment
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/13296> ("Drop or replace turtles"), filed by Damian Johnson: *"Mike lacks either the time or interest in maintaining turtles… We have a directory authority that's so unmaintained I've given up even reporting problems with it any longer."* Resolution: **turtles removed, longclaw added, "This keeps the directory authority count at 9."** Faravahar also got a new IP (ticket 14487). turtles' v3ident was `27B6B5996C426270A5C95488AA5BCEB6BCC86956` (confirmed against `src/or/config.c` at tag `tor-0.2.3.25`). Its last certificate — still served today in `/tor/keys/all` — was published 2014-07-09 and expired 2014-10-09. Confidence: **high**.

### 2014-12-19 — public warning of imminent **seizure of directory authorities**
<https://blog.torproject.org/possible-upcoming-attempts-disable-tor-network/> (arma): *"there may be an attempt to incapacitate our network in the next few days through the seizure of specialized servers in the network called directory authorities."* Updates: `[Dec 22: So far all is quiet on the directory authority front]`, `[Dec 28: Still quiet.]` **Nothing happened.** Confidence: high (that nothing happened is asserted by Tor's own updates; I found no later contradiction).

### 2015-11-18 → 2016-01-07 — **dannenberg's v3 identity key changed and Tor didn't notice for ~7 weeks**
This is the sharpest operational lesson in the whole file. Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/17906> ("Dannenberg's v3ident needs to change"), opened 2015-12-20 by Damian Johnson, closed 2016-01-07:

> *"Dannenberg's v3ident has changed and this needs to be updated in config.c. Trivial change, but this should be corrected soonish since **due to it Dannenberg isn't taking part in the consensus**…"*

The ticket includes a live `stem` dump of the consensus signers at the time showing **eight** authorities and **dannenberg absent**, plus the PGP-signed mail Andreas Lehner sent to `dir-auth@` on **2015-11-18** announcing the new v3ident `0232AF901C31A04EE9848595AF9BB7620D4C5B2E`. Old value: `585769C78764D58426B8B52B6651A5A71137189A` (verified against `src/or/config.c` at tags `tor-0.2.3.25`, `tor-0.2.5.12`, `tor-0.2.6.10`). **The identity key was rotated out-of-band by PGP mail on a private list; the hardcoded root of trust in the client lagged by weeks; and during that window the network effectively ran at 8 signers.** Confidence: **very high**.

### 2015-11-24 → 2016-04-14 — **moria1 rolled its signing cert and could not compute a consensus at all**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/17668>. Roger: *"On moria1, my v3 cert was going to expire soon, so I made a new one."* Result, every hour:
```
[warn] ID on signature on network-status vote does not match any declared directory source.
[err]  networkstatus_compute_consensus(): Bug: Generated a networkstatus consensus we couldn't parse.
[warn] Couldn't generate a ns consensus at all!
[warn] Couldn't generate any consensus flavors at all.
```
*"I wonder if we broke 'moving to a new certificate' somewhere in there?"* — **the routine, prescribed, once-a-year signing-key rollover broke the implementation.** Confidence: **very high**.

### 2016 (0.2.8.5-rc) — **urras** removed
`ReleaseNotes`: *"Urras is no longer a directory authority. Closes ticket 19271."* Ticket 19271 returns null from the API (migration gap), so I could not recover the stated reason. Its v3ident was `80550987E1D626E3EBA5E5E75A458DE0626D088C`; last cert published 2015-11-10, expired 2016-11-10. Confidence: high on the fact, **none on the reason**.

### 2016-09-01 — **Tonga** bridge authority retired and **its keys destroyed**
<https://blog.torproject.org/new-bridge-authority/> (isis): after ten years, Lucky Green stepped down. *"**Tonga's cryptographic keys will be destroyed this week.**"* Replaced by **Bifröst** (hardware/hosting donated by Greenhost). Relay operators running bridges had to upgrade to 0.2.8.7 / 0.2.9.2-alpha or *"cease to be distributed to new clients."* Note the post's own framing: *"Unlike the other directory authorities, the Bridge Authority does not get a vote in Tor's consensus protocol."* (Today's bridge authority in `auth_dirs.inc` is **Serge**, `BA44A889E64B93FAA2B114E02C2A279A8555C533`, flagged `bridge`, **no `v3ident`** — so it does not count toward quorum.) Confidence: **very high**.

### 2017 (0.3.0.x / 0.3.2.x) — **Bastet** added as a ninth authority; longclaw changed IP and lost then regained IPv6
Changelog: *"Add 'Bastet' as a ninth directory authority to the default list. Closes ticket 23910."*, *"The directory authority 'Longclaw' has changed its IP address. Closes ticket 23592."*, *"Remove longclaw's IPv6 address, as it will soon change… This leaves 3/8 directory authorities with IPv6 addresses."* Confidence: high.

### 2020-01-21 → 2020-05-21 — **dirauths saturated at 400+ Mbit/s; consensus formation degraded**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/33018> ("Dir auths using an unsustainable 400+ mbit/s, need to diagnose and fix"), Roger:

> *"**We've been having problems establishing a consensus lately.** We realized that maatuska was rate limiting to only 10MBytes/s… today we realized that **moria1 was unable to serve dirport answers because it was maxed out at its BandwidthRate of 30MBytes.** I raised that to 50MBytes and it stayed maxed out."*

Companion ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/33072> (still **open**, filed 2020-01-27): *"most of the problematic requests are coming from aggressively non-tor sources"* → proposal to 503 uncompressed dirport requests. This is the closest thing to "the 2020 DoS on dirauths" I could substantiate: an **overload**, source characterized as non-Tor clients, not attributed to a named attacker. Confidence: **high** on the facts, **low** on it being a deliberate DoS.

### 2021-01-10 — **the network went several rounds with no consensus; v3 onion services broke network-wide**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40237> (opened and closed 2021-01-10 → 2021-01-12), Roger:

> *"This bug got exposed today because **the network went several rounds without a consensus** due to the ongoing issues of #33018 and #33072, and thus Tor clients and Tor onion services ended up with a consensus that still worked (it was made within the past 24 hours), but was no longer considered 'live'. So normal Tor circuits (using exit relays) still worked, and v2 onion services still worked, but **v3 onion services stopped working for that time period** — services wouldn't publish descriptors, and clients wouldn't fetch them."*

Log line: `[warn] No live consensus so we can't get the responsible hidden service directories.`

**This is the January 2021 consensus failure.** Its root cause is the *same* dirauth overload as #33018. Two follow-ons:
- <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40239> (2021-01-10, closed 2022-05-20): Roger, Sebastian and Sina ran an experimental patch making authorities answer **503 "Directory voting, try again later"** to consensus and microdesc fetches from `:48` to `:59.5` each hour, to clear bandwidth for voting.
- <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40252> (2021-01-20 → 2021-01-29): *"Dir auths should try for a consensus on the :30 as soon as the :00 failed."* Currently, if a consensus exists within the last 3 hours the authorities only vote hourly; the fix makes them fall back to twice-hourly on the first miss. *"The overall goal here is to reduce the frequency that we have no live consensus."*

Confidence: **very high** that a multi-round consensus failure occurred on/around 2021-01-10. **I could not find a published post-mortem or an exact list of the missed intervals.**

### 2021-02-13 — a consensus apparently shipped **with no exit nodes**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40292> — still **open**, titled *"Mystery: Every relay and bridge said '[notice] The current consensus has no exit nodes.' this morning."* Relays logged `We have 0/6708` microdescriptors and `0% of path bw (no exits in consensus)`. Roger: *"the first and biggest question is: did that actually happen as described? Like, was there a consensus with no exits?"* **Never resolved.** Confidence: high that it was reported and unresolved; **unknown** whether a no-exit consensus actually formed.

### 2022-03-02 (ongoing) — **moria1's IP blackholed from parts of the internet**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/network-health/analysis/-/issues/10>, still **open**:

> *"UPDATE: **the entire 128.31.0.39 IP address is blackholed from some parts of the internet.** So it is not a particular port being overwhelmed — there is a router in the middle of the internet that has a 'drop' rule for this IP address!"*

Consensus-health alerts: `ERROR: Unable to retrieve the consensus from moria1`. Roger's note on why it matters: *"relays, including dir auths, use the (lighter weight) dirport to fetch new dir info and to post votes and signatures."* At the time: *"I am not seeing problems with the hourly votes yet, so it is not critical, but it is definitely an anomaly."* An authority partially unreachable from the public internet, for years, with no clean remedy. Confidence: **very high**.

### 2022-10 to 2024-05 — **Faravahar removed; the network ran on 8 authorities for ~19 months**
- <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40687> (2022-10-18→26): dizum's operator moved it from AS47482 to AS61125 → `45.66.33.45` → `45.66.35.11`.
- <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40688> (2022-10-18→26): *"Remove `Faravahar` from the code until its operator can bring it back online **outside Team Cymru's network**."* Shipped in **0.4.7.11, 2022-11-10**. Commit `efad436432`, David Goulet.
- <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40689>: *"Add new Faravahar 2.0 back to code"* — open **2022-10-19 → 2024-05-30**. Commit `39ba9ce0d7`: *"dirauth: Add new faravahar — Brand new address, brand new keys, brand new day."* Shipped in **0.4.8.12, 2024-06-06**.

Old Faravahar v3ident `EFCBE720AB3A82B99F9E953CD5BF50F7EEFC7B97`; new one `70849B868D606BAECFB6128C5E3D782029AA394F`. **A dirauth was removed for a hosting-provider trust concern (Team Cymru) and its seat sat empty for nineteen months.** Confidence: **very high**.

### 2022-11/12 — **moria1 key rotation after a physical break-in**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40722> (2022-11-30 → 2022-12-06), Roger:

> *"We don't have any specific evidence that the old keys are compromised, but they've been online almost a decade, and also **there was a break-in to the old hardware this month (November 2022)** so it is better to do it and be safe."*

Commit `72b04a5aa4`: *"Rotate the relay identity key and v3 identity key for moria1. They have been online for more than a decade, **there was a known potential compromise**, and anyway refreshing keys periodically is good practice."* (Note: the commit message says "known potential compromise"; the shipped changelog softened this to just "refreshing keys periodically is good practice." Worth noticing as a disclosure-practice datum.) Old v3ident `D586D18309DED4CD6D57C18FDB97EFA96D330566` → new `F533C81CEF0BC0267857C99B2F471ADF249FA232`. Shipped 0.4.7.12 / 0.4.5.15, **2022-12-06**. Confidence: **very high**.

### 2022-12-01 — **the roster change split the quorum in the wild**
This is the best real-world demonstration of exactly how the threshold works. Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/40725>, *"[warn] Received http status code 404 ('Consensus not signed by sufficient number of requested authorities')"*, Roger:

> *"some relays still believe in Faravahar as a directory authority, so if they get a consensus with five signatures including one from Faravahar they accept it and serve it; whereas modern Tor clients no longer believe Faravahar is one of the directory authorities, so from their perspective **that consensus only has four acceptable signatures on it**, so they decline to download it… as long as most relays are serving a consensus with six or more sigs on it, then everything should be fine."*
> *"[edit: Sebastian correctly points out that while we're seeing this issue with Faravahar's key currently, **we could see it after Tuesday with moria1's new key as well**.]"*

**Because the threshold is computed against the *client's own* configured roster, not the roster listed in the consensus, two Tor versions disagreeing about who the authorities are produces a partition where the same document is over-threshold for one population and under-threshold for the other.** Confidence: **very high**.

### 2024-04-08 — **tor26 new keys and address**
Commit `e3e4fa35e8` (David Goulet), *"dirauth: New keys and address for tor26"* — no ticket, no reason given anywhere I could find. Old v3ident `14C131DFC5C6F93646BE72FA1401C02A8DF2E8B4` (that cert expired 2024-11-01), new `2F3DF9CA0E5D36F2685A2DA67184EB8DCB8CBA8C`. **Reason: unknown.** Confidence: high on the fact, **zero** on the why.

### 2026-08-21 — live bug: a rogue authority can **sabotage the shared random value**
Ticket <https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/issues/41356>, **open**: *"Dir auth can imitate somebody else's SRV commit to sabotage consensus SRV."* `compare_reveal_()` in `src/feature/dirauth/shared_random.c` sorts only on `hashed_reveal` with **no tiebreaker**, though the code comment claims the list is ordered by authority fingerprint per proposal 250. A hostile authority X can insert two commits with identical `hashed_reveal` and make different authorities compute different SRVs → consensus divergence. Requires a rogue dirauth. Confidence: high (quoting the ticket).

---

# 3. WHAT THE SPEC MERELY PRESCRIBES

## 3.1 Authority identity key vs signing key

<https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/outline.html>:

> *"Every authority has a very-secret, long-term **'Authority Identity Key'**. **This is stored encrypted and/or offline**, and is used to sign 'key certificate' documents. Every key certificate contains a **medium-term (3-12 months) 'authority signing key'**, that is used by the authority to sign other directory information. (Note that the authority identity key is distinct from the router identity key that the authority uses in its role as an ordinary router.)"*

And the stated motivation, same page, "What's different from version 2?": *"Authorities used to sign documents using the same private keys they used for their roles as routers. **This forced them to keep these extremely sensitive keys in memory unencrypted.**"* The split traces to **proposal 103, "Splitting identity key from regularly used signing key"** (listed at the top of <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/index.html>).

Certificate structure — <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/creating-key-certificates.html>:

| Item | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `dir-key-certificate-version 3` | MUST be 3 |
| `fingerprint` | `SHA1(DER(KP_auth_id_rsa))`, uppercase hex |
| `dir-key-published` / `dir-key-expires` | cert validity window |
| `dir-identity-key` | long-term `KP_auth_id_rsa`; SHOULD be ≥2048 bits, MUST NOT be <1024 |
| `dir-signing-key` | medium-term `KP_auth_sign_rsa`; MUST be ≥1024 bits |
| `dir-key-crosscert` | signature *by the signing key* over `SHA1(DER(KP_auth_id_rsa))` — wrapped in `BEGIN ID SIGNATURE` for compat with a broken parser; the spec itself carries an inline `TODO: what is dir-key-crosscert for?` |
| `dir-key-certification` | RSA signature of the whole cert **by the identity key** — the only place the offline key is used |

Rules: *"Authorities MUST generate a new signing key and corresponding certificate before the key expires."* And the anti-retroactive-invalidation rule: *"A vote must contain an authority certificate which is valid for the whole relevant consensus validity period… This means a vote can't retrospectively become invalid because its authcert expires: either the authcert is considered OK for the whole time when the vote might be relevant, or it was never valid."*

## 3.2 `tor-gencert` — the actual tool

Manpage: <https://manpages.debian.org/unstable/tor/tor-gencert.1.en.html> (checked against tor 0.4.9.11-1).

```
tor-gencert [-h|--help] [-v] [-r|--reuse] [--create-identity-key]
            [-i id_file] [-c cert_file] [-m num] [-a address:port]
            [-s signing_key_file] [--passphrase-fd FILEDES]
```

> *"Every directory authority has a long term authority identity key (which is distinct from the identity key it uses as a Tor server); **this key should be kept offline in a secure location**. It is used to certify shorter-lived signing keys, which are kept online and used by the directory authority to sign votes and consensus documents. After you use this program to generate a signing key and a certificate, copy those files to the keys subdirectory of your Tor process, and send Tor a SIGHUP signal. **DO NOT COPY THE IDENTITY KEY.**"*

- `-m NUM` — *"Number of months that the certificate should be valid. **Default: 12.**"*
- `--create-identity-key` — *"You should only use this option the first time you run tor-gencert."*
- `-r/--reuse` — new certificate, same signing key (for changing address or lifetime).
- `--passphrase-fd` — identity key is passphrase-encrypted; default reads from terminal.
- Defaults: `./authority_identity_key`, `./authority_signing_key`, `./authority_certificate`.
- BUGS section: *"This probably doesn't run on Windows. That's not a big issue, since we don't really want authorities to be running on Windows anyway."*

**Related torrc options** (from `tor(1)`, <https://manpages.debian.org/unstable/tor/tor.1.en.html>):
- `V3AuthUseLegacyKey 0|1` — *"the directory authority will sign consensuses not only with its own signing key, but also with a 'legacy' key and certificate with a different identity. **This feature is used to migrate directory authority keys in the event of a compromise.** (Default: 0)"* Backed by `KeyDirectory/legacy_certificate` and `KeyDirectory/legacy_signing_key`.
- `V3AuthVotingInterval` default **1 hour**; `V3AuthVoteDelay` and `V3AuthDistDelay` default **5 minutes** each; `V3AuthNIntervalsValid` default **3** (min 2) — *"Choosing high numbers increases network partitioning risks; choosing low numbers increases directory traffic."*

**Practice vs. spec — measured.** I pulled the live cert bundle from `http://171.25.193.9:443/tor/keys/all` on 2026-09-03:

| v3ident (prefix) | Authority | Published | Expires | Lifetime |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `49015F78` | maatuska | 2026-06-16 | 2026-09-16 | **3 mo** |
| `2F3DF9CA` | tor26 | 2026-06-01 | 2027-02-01 | 8 mo |
| `0232AF90` | dannenberg | 2026-02-15 | 2027-02-15 | 12 mo |
| `F533C81C` | moria1 | 2026-03-29 | 2027-03-29 | 12 mo |
| `ED03BB61` | gabelmoo | 2026-01-09 | 2027-01-09 | 12 mo |
| `23D15D96` | longclaw | 2026-08-10 | 2027-08-10 | 12 mo |
| `27102BC1` | bastet | 2026-07-25 | 2027-07-25 | 12 mo |
| `70849B86` | faravahar | 2026-05-29 | 2027-05-29 | 12 mo |
| `E8A9C45E` | **dizum** | 2026-03-21 | **2027-09-21** | **18 mo** |

**dizum's certificate lifetime (18 months) exceeds the spec's stated "3-12 months" range.** Nothing enforces the upper bound — the spec only says implementations SHOULD reject certs published too far in the future, and MUST NOT accept expired ones. Confidence: **high** (I read the bytes off the wire; my nickname↔v3ident mapping comes from `auth_dirs.inc`).

Also: `/tor/keys/all` **still serves long-dead certificates** — turtles (`27B6B599`, expired 2014-10-09), dannenberg's pre-2015 key (`585769C7`, expired 2016-04-13), urras (`80550987`, expired 2016-11-10), old Faravahar (`EFCBE720`, expired 2023-02-08), old moria1 (`D586D183`, expired 2023-11-10), old tor26 (`14C131DF`, expired 2024-11-01). The retired-key graveyard is publicly served, which is a useful forensic artifact.

## 3.3 Relay ed25519 master identity key vs medium-term signing key

Design landed in **0.2.7.x, 2015**, ticket 13642: *"Add support for offline encrypted Ed25519 master keys. To use this feature on your tor relay, run 'tor --keygen' to make a new master key."* Ticket 16944 added `OfflineMasterKey`; ticket 16769 added `--newpass`.

Files (`tor(1)`, FILES section):
- `KeyDirectory/ed25519_master_id_secret_key` — *"This key is used to sign the medium-term ed25519 signing key. **This file can be kept offline or encrypted. If so, Tor will not be able to generate new signing keys automatically; you'll need to use `tor --keygen` to do so.**"*
- `KeyDirectory/ed25519_signing_secret_key` — the online medium-term key.
- `KeyDirectory/ed25519_signing_cert` — binds the two.

Options:
- **`SigningKeyLifetime N days|weeks|months` — Default: 30 days.** ✅ Your 30-day figure is correct.
- `OfflineMasterKey 0|1` — *"the Tor relay will never generate or load its master secret key. Instead, you'll have to use 'tor --keygen' to manage the permanent ed25519 master identity key, as well as the corresponding temporary signing keys and certificates. (Default: 0)"*
- `TestingSigningKeySlop` — how early before expiry to replace: **3 hours for link/auth, 1 day for signing**.
- `tor --key-expiration sign [--format iso8601|timestamp]` — prints `signing-cert-expiry: …`, for cron-driven rotation.

Rollover logic, `src/feature/relay/routerkeys.c` (<https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/raw/main/src/feature/relay/routerkeys.c>), lines ~136–195: `need_new_signing_key` is set when there's no key, the cert `EXPIRES_SOON(…, 0)`, or `--keygen` was given; `want_new_signing_key` widens that by `TestingSigningKeySlop`. When `OfflineMasterKey` is set, the log says: *"It looks like I should try to generate and sign a new medium-term signing key… **But OfflineMasterKey is set, so I won't try to load a permanent master identity key. You will need to use 'tor --keygen' to make a new signing key and certificate.**"* Notably, `offline_master` is `options->OfflineMasterKey && options->command != CMD_KEYGEN` — the flag suppresses master-key loading during normal operation but explicitly not during `--keygen`.

### ⚠️ **`--keygen-force` DOES NOT EXIST.**
Confidence: **very high**. I checked three independent places:
- `src/app/config/config.c` command-line table (<…/raw/main/src/app/config/config.c>) lists exactly `--keygen`, `--keygen-family`, `--keygen-onion`, `--newpass`, `--no-passphrase`, `--passphrase-fd`.
- `src/app/main/main.c` handles `CMD_KEYGEN`, `CMD_KEYGEN_FAMILY`, `CMD_KEYGEN_ONION` only.
- `grep -c "keygen-force"` over the **full 41,957-line ChangeLog** → **0**.

The flag you want is plain `--keygen`, which regenerates the signing key unconditionally (`options->command == CMD_KEYGEN && !options->change_key_passphrase` forces `need_new_signing_key`). `--keygen-family` (family ID keys) and `--keygen-onion` are the other two; `--keygen-family` is recent (bug 41184, bugfix on 0.4.9.1-alpha).

**Also flagging:** I could not find a dedicated operator how-to for offline master keys. `https://community.torproject.org/relay/setup/post-install/offline-keys/` is **404**; the post-install page only *mentions* Offline Master Keys in passing (recommending Ansible Relayor for the manual steps). The old how-to lived at `trac.torproject.org/projects/tor/wiki/doc/TorRelaySecurity/OfflineKeys` (Wayback has a 2020-10-21 snapshot at `http://web.archive.org/web/20201021072708/…`, which I could not retrieve — the Internet Archive was serving "Temporarily Offline" during this session). **The current authoritative operator documentation for relay offline master keys is the `tor(1)` manpage and nothing else.** Similarly, I found **no** "Tor directory authority operator" handbook anywhere on `torproject.org`, `community.torproject.org`, or in `doc/` in the tor repo — the dirauth operator procedure appears to live entirely on the private `dir-auth@` list (see the dannenberg incident, where a key rotation was announced by PGP-signed mail to that list).

## 3.4 Quorum and the consensus-signature threshold

**Client-side rule — the one that actually matters.** `src/feature/nodelist/networkstatus.c`, `networkstatus_check_consensus_signature()` (<https://gitlab.torproject.org/tpo/core/tor/-/raw/main/src/feature/nodelist/networkstatus.c>, line 527):

```c
int n_v3_authorities = get_n_authorities(V3_DIRINFO);
int n_required = n_v3_authorities/2 + 1;
```

Return values: `1` if every recognized authority signed, `0` if `n_good >= n_required`, `-1` if fetching missing certs could get there, `-2` otherwise. The user-facing message is literally: *"A consensus needs %d good signatures from recognized authorities for us to accept it. This %s one has %d (%s)."*

Three consequences that people get wrong:
- **The denominator is the client's own configured authority list, not the number of `dir-source` lines in the consensus.** `get_n_authorities(V3_DIRINFO)` counts trusted dir servers the client knows. This is precisely what produced the December 2022 split (§ above).
- **With 9 authorities today, the threshold is 5.** Under 8 (Nov 2022 – Jun 2024) it was also 5. Under 6 (2008) it was 4.
- Only one signature per authority counts: *"note that only one signature from each authority should be 'counted' as meaning that the authority has signed the consensus."*

**Publication-side rule** — <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/publishing-consensus.html>: *"The voting period ends at the valid-after time. **If the consensus has been signed by a majority of authorities**, these documents are made available at `/tor/status-vote/current/consensus`."*

**Vote-computation thresholds** — <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/computing-consensus.html>. Note these are *content* thresholds, distinct from the signature threshold, and the denominator is **total authorities, not just those who voted**:
- flags, `<Ed,RSA>` identity inclusion: *"more than half of the total authorities (not just total votes)"*
- consensus params: *"a majority of authorities (**total authorities, not just those participating in this vote**) voted on, or if at least three authorities voted for that parameter"* — value is the **low-median**
- recommended versions: *"more than half of the voting authorities that included a [version list]"*
- BadExit: *"more than half of the authorities who advertise [an opinion]"*
- protocol support: *"only if it is listed by a majority of the voters. A majority of votes is sufficient to make a protocol un-supported. **A supermajority of authorities (2/3)**…"* [required]
- all ties broken *"in favor of the smaller or earlier item"*

**Consensus-method negotiation** — same page:

> *"Before generating a consensus, an authority must decide which consensus method to use. To do this, it looks for the **highest version number supported by more than 2/3 of the authorities voting**. If it supports this method, then it uses it. Otherwise, it falls back to the newest consensus method that it supports (**which will probably not result in a sufficiently signed consensus**)."*

Each vote carries `consensus-methods M M M…` (absence ⇒ only method 1); the resulting consensus carries a single `consensus-method M`. Current state: **all authorities MUST support method 25**; SHOULD NOT advertise anything before 25; method **21 is explicitly blacklisted** (*"Did not correctly enable support for ed25519 key collation"*). **Methods 37–99 are reserved for CTor; 100 is the first Arti method** (*"Do not include relays without a canonical Ed identity"*). The live network today runs `consensus-method 35`. Method **3** is *"Added legacy ID key support to aid in authority ID key rollovers"* — the on-the-wire half of `V3AuthUseLegacyKey`.

**The voting timeline** — <https://spec.torproject.org/dir-spec/outline.html>. VA−DistSeconds−VoteSeconds: exchange votes. VA−DistSeconds−VoteSeconds/2: fetch missing votes; *"Authorities SHOULD also reject any votes that other authorities try to upload after this time. (0.4.4.1-alpha was the first version to reject votes in this way.)"* with an explicit rationale worth quoting:

> *"Refusing late uploaded votes minimizes the chance of a **consensus split**… If an authority is struggling to upload its vote, and finally uploads to a fraction of authorities after this period, they will compute a consensus different from the others… Rejecting late uploaded votes **does not fix the problem entirely.** If some authorities are able to download a specific vote, but others fail to do so, then there may still be a consensus split."*

VA−DistSeconds: compute consensus, exchange signatures. VA: all authorities have a multiply-signed consensus. VU+24h: clients stop using it entirely (`NETWORKSTATUS_ALLOW_SKEW = 24*60*60`; *"a 'reasonably live' consensus is one that expired less than 24 hours ago"*). That 24-hour grace is exactly the window that kept ordinary circuits working — but not v3 onion services — during the January 2021 failure.

